{"id": "Q1", "flags": [{"check": "attitude_object", "stage": "comprehension", "level": "consider", "quote": "the council for the area where you live now", "why": "If more than one council covers a respondent's home, they may not know which one is meant. The analyst should check whether this applies."}], "rewrite": ""},
{"id": "Q2", "flags": [{"check": "specific_concrete", "stage": "comprehension", "level": "fix", "quote": "do its job well", "why": "\"Its job\" is not defined. Respondents will think of different council tasks, so answers will not be about the same thing."}], "rewrite": "Your local council is the council for the area where you live now. Some people think it runs local services well and others think it does not. How much do you trust your local council to run local services well?\nNo trust at all / A little trust / A moderate amount of trust / A lot of trust / Complete trust / I don't have an opinion"},
{"id": "Q3", "flags": [], "rewrite": ""},
{"id": "Q4", "flags": [{"check": "specific_concrete", "stage": "comprehension", "level": "fix", "quote": "put its own interests ahead of residents' interests", "why": "Unclear whose interests count (staff, councillors, the organisation) and which residents. Respondents must guess, and \"interests\" is abstract."}, {"check": "construct_first", "stage": "judgment", "level": "consider", "quote": "How often does your local council put its own interests ahead of residents' interests?", "why": "This asks how often the council acts selfishly, not how much the respondent trusts it. The analyst should decide whether it fits the construct and how to score it."}, {"check": "specific_concrete", "stage": "retrieval", "level": "consider", "quote": "Sometimes", "why": "The question gives no events to recall, so respondents must generalise about the whole council. \"Rarely\", \"Sometimes\" and \"Often\" will mean different amounts to different people."}], "rewrite": "Your local council is the council for the area where you live now. Some people think it makes decisions that benefit residents and others think it makes decisions that benefit the council itself. How often does your local council make decisions that benefit the council more than residents?\nNever / Rarely / Sometimes / Often / Always / I don't have an opinion"}
]}
